Description:

httpd can accept TLS 1.3 early data (0-RTT) on HTTPS connections, over
HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, so a client resuming an earlier session gets its
first answers a round trip sooner.  It's off by default
(http_allow_0rtt), because early data can be replayed: with it on, each
resumption ticket is good for only one connection, and an early request
with an unsafe method gets 425 Too Early (RFC 8470), as does one an
intermediary marks "Early-Data: 1".

Supporting this, tls_eccurve now takes a list of key exchange groups,
so most TLS 1.3 clients no longer need an extra round trip on every
handshake, and a client that closes a connection without close_notify
no longer loses the session it would resume.


Documentation:

The new http_allow_0rtt option and the tls_eccurve list are documented,
and RFC 8470 is now listed as supported.


Config changes:

Added http_allow_0rtt, off by default.  tls_eccurve accepts a
colon-separated list, and its default changed from prime256v1 to
X25519MLKEM768:X25519:prime256v1:secp384r1.


Upgrade instructions:

A tls_eccurve setting naming a single curve keeps working, still limited
to that curve; remove it to get the new default.  With http_allow_0rtt
on, tls_session_timeout must not be 0, and a TLS 1.2 client can resume a
session only once.


